## Pass/Fail Criteria

**PASS — All pipelines healthy:**
- All CM tables (flow_stats, histogram_stats, agg_flow_stats, congestion_stats, vcmts_sfid_info) have MAX(time) within the last 5 minutes
- All vCMTS tables have MAX(timestamp) within the last 5 minutes
- All tables have row counts > 0
- SFID mapping returns valid us/ds service flow names for a known modem
- Latency, AQM drops, and sanctioned packet tables show data per modem/direction/sf_index
- Polling intervals are consistent (~15-30 seconds)

**FAIL — Pipeline issue detected:**
- Any table has 0 rows
- Any vCMTS table MAX(timestamp) is older than 5 minutes
- Any CM table MAX(time) is older than 24 hours (known issue — CM pipeline may have stopped)
- SFID mapping returns no results for a registered modem
- Polling intervals show large gaps (> 2 minutes between samples)

## Known Issues
- **CM metrics pipeline stoppage**: The CM metrics tables have previously stopped collecting around March 24th. If Step 2 shows stale MAX(time), escalate to investigate the collection pipeline.
- **qos_service_flow_packets/octets are NOT data-plane**: These counters only show management traffic (~28KB during a 421Mbps test). Do not use for throughput validation.
- **MAC format**: Raw psql queries require colon-separated lowercase MAC (e.g. 0c:b9:37:64:3a:b0). Convert from Cisco notation (0cb9.3764.3ab0) manually.
- **Polling interval**: vCMTS metrics poll every ~15 seconds. Samples appear at ~30s intervals in the DB.

## Notes
- Replace `<cm_mac_colon_format>` with the actual cable modem MAC in colon-separated lowercase format (e.g. 0c:b9:37:64:3a:b0)
- All commands are run from aa-ap-sandbox: `ssh aphillips@aa-ap-sandbox`
- Step 11 is the quickest way to check pipeline health — run it first, then drill into specific tables if something looks stale